      <CodeBlock code={"apiVersion: v1\nkind: ConfigMap\nmetadata:\n  name: user-workload-monitoring-config\n  namespace: openshift-user-workload-monitoring\ndata:\n  config.yaml: |"} lang="yaml" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Apply the <code>2_configure_user_workload_monitoring.yaml</code> file by using the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc apply -f 2_configure_user_workload_monitoring.yaml\nconfigmap/user-workload-monitoring-config created"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      </ol>
      <h2 id="oadp-creating-service-monitor_oadp-monitoring" class="group scroll-mt-20">Creating OADP service monitor<a href="#oadp-creating-service-monitor_oadp-monitoring" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <p class="_abstract">Create a <code>ServiceMonitor</code> resource to scrape Velero metrics from the OADP service endpoint. This helps you collect metrics for monitoring backup and restore operations in the OpenShift Container Platform monitoring stack.</p>
      <p>OADP provides an <code>openshift-adp-velero-metrics-svc</code> service. The user workload monitoring service monitor must use the <code>openshift-adp-velero-metrics-svc</code> service.</p>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      Ensure that the <code>openshift-adp-velero-metrics-svc</code> service exists. It should contain <code>app.kubernetes.io/name=velero</code> label, which is used as selector for the <code>ServiceMonitor</code> object.
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get svc -n openshift-adp -l app.kubernetes.io/name=velero"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"NAME                               TYPE        CLUSTER-IP      EXTERNAL-IP   PORT(S)    AGE\nopenshift-adp-velero-metrics-svc   ClusterIP   172.30.38.244   <none>        8085/TCP   1h"} lang="terminal" title="Example output" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Create a <code>ServiceMonitor</code> YAML file that matches the existing service label, and save the file as <code>3_create_oadp_service_monitor.yaml</code>. The service monitor is created in the <code>openshift-adp</code> namespace which has the <code>openshift-adp-velero-metrics-svc</code> service.
      <CodeBlock code={"apiVersion: monitoring.coreos.com/v1\nkind: ServiceMonitor\nmetadata:\n  labels:\n    app: oadp-service-monitor\n  name: oadp-service-monitor\n  namespace: openshift-adp\nspec:\n  endpoints:\n  - interval: 30s\n    path: /metrics\n    targetPort: 8085\n    scheme: http\n  selector:\n    matchLabels:\n      app.kubernetes.io/name: \"velero\""} lang="yaml" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Apply the <code>3_create_oadp_service_monitor.yaml</code> file:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc apply -f 3_create_oadp_service_monitor.yaml"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"servicemonitor.monitoring.coreos.com/oadp-service-monitor created"} lang="terminal" title="Example output" />
      </li>
      </ol>
      <p><strong>Verification</strong></p>
      <ul>
      <li>
      Confirm that the new service monitor is in an <strong>Up</strong> state by using the <strong>Administrator</strong> perspective of the OpenShift Container Platform web console. Wait a few minutes for the service monitor to reach the <strong>Up</strong> state.
      <ol>
      <li>Navigate to the <strong>Observe</strong> -&gt; <strong>Targets</strong> page.</li>
      <li>Ensure the <strong>Filter</strong> is unselected or that the <strong>User</strong> source is selected and type <code>openshift-adp</code> in the <code>Text</code> search field.</li>
      <li>
      Verify that the status for the <strong>Status</strong> for the service monitor is <strong>Up</strong>.
